{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HkFHGzLJD8Dd9h3zoDwQy2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture static probe (32B models)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Layer sweep + static LogisticRegression on r1-32b (Qwen2.5-base, 64 layers) and QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5-base, 64 layers). Extracts all 64 layers, finds optimal via Fisher discriminant + probe accuracy, reports static coverage.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"r1_32b_best_layer_idx\": 28,\n7\t      \"r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.44,\n8\t      \"r1_32b_static_acc\": 0.824,\n9\t      \"r1_32b_static_coverage\": 0.42,\n10\t      \"r1_32b_static_lead_mean\": -1.8,\n11\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig\": 19.3,\n12\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3\": 1.05,\n13\t      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_idx\": 30,\n14\t      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.47,\n15\t      \"qwq_32b_static_acc\": 0.811,\n16\t      \"qwq_32b_static_coverage\": 0.39,\n17\t      \"qwq_32b_static_lead_mean\": -2.3,\n18\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig\": 18.7,\n19\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3\": 0.98,\n20\t      \"var_explained_3pc_r1_32b\": 0.14,\n21\t      \"var_explained_3pc_qwq\": 0.15\n22\t    },\n23\t    \"tables\": [\n24\t      \"Table: Layer sweep summary (top-5 layers per model by probe accuracy, with Fisher ratios)\",\n25\t      \"Table: Static probe metrics comparison across all 3 models (r1-8b, r1-32b, QwQ-32B)\"\n26\t    ],\n27\t    \"figures\": [],\n28\t    \"analysis\": \"Static probes replicate the r1-8b pattern on Qwen2.5-base architecture: high Fisher discriminant in full representation space but collapse in low-dimensional projections. The ~44% depth optimum generalizes, consistent with middle layers encoding task-relevant abstractions before output-head specialization. Static coverage 0.39-0.42 confirms Claim 1 cross-architecture: single-step linear probes are fundamentally limited to <50% coverage regardless of model scale or architecture family.\\n\\nBest layer at ~44% depth replicates r1-8b finding (layer 14/32 = 44%). Static coverage 0.39-0.42 mirrors r1-8b's 0.465 with slight degradation expected from larger model's more distributed representations. Fisher discriminant remains high in full space (18-19) but collapses in PCA-3D (~1.0), confirming the safety signal is distributed across minor principal components regardless of architecture. Qwen2.5-base models show same phenomenon as Llama-base r1-8b because the distributional property is a consequence of safety fine-tuning spreading signal across dimensions, not architecture-specific.\"\n29\t  },\n30\t  {\n31\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\",\n32\t    \"description\": \"Temporal LR (W=15) on best-layer hidden states from r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Validates that +30pp coverage gap over static replicates cross-architecture.\",\n33\t    \"results\": {\n34\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.77,\n35\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.856,\n36\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.6,\n37\t      \"r1_32b_coverage_gap_over_static_pp\": 35,\n38\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.74,\n39\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.843,\n40\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.9,\n41\t      \"qwq_32b_coverage_gap_over_static_pp\": 35,\n42\t      \"mean_cross_arch_temporal_coverage\": 0.755,\n43\t      \"mean_cross_arch_gap_pp\": 35\n44\t    },\n45\t    \"tables\": [\n46\t      \"Table: Temporal vs static coverage/accuracy/lead-time across all 3 models\"\n47\t    ],\n48\t    \"figures\": [],\n49\t    \"analysis\": \"The ≥25pp temporal advantage replicates on both Qwen2.5-base models, confirming Claim 2 is architecture-general. Coverage values (0.74-0.77) cluster near the r1-8b oracle ceiling (76.7%), suggesting the ~77% bound may reflect an intrinsic property of the commitment detection task (some traces have ambiguous/gradual commitment without clean phase transition). The result validates that temporal context — not model capacity or architecture — is the critical missing ingredient for static probes.\\n\\nTemporal window W=15 captures 'safety reasoning density decline' — the gradual thinning of safety-labeled steps before commitment point. This pattern is architectural-invariant because it arises from the shared safety fine-tuning objective: all models learn to reason about safety similarly, producing similar temporal density patterns. The +35pp gap over static probes occurs because the commitment signature is fundamentally a trajectory property (declining safety density over 10-15 steps), not a single-step state property. r1-32b slightly outperforms QwQ due to deeper safety fine-tuning in the DeepSeek-R1 pipeline, but both saturate near the oracle ceiling (76.7% on r1-8b), suggesting the oracle bound is also architecture-invariant.\"\n50\t  },\n51\t  {\n52\t    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer (Llama↔Qwen)\",\n53\t    \"description\": \"Train temporal probe on r1-8b (Llama-3.1, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5, 5120-d) via learned linear projection head. Also reverse direction.\",\n54\t    \"results\": {\n55\t      \"llama_to_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.54,\n56\t      \"llama_to_r1_32b_retention_pct\": 0.7,\n57\t      \"llama_to_qwq_coverage\": 0.51,\n58\t      \"llama_to_qwq_retention_pct\": 0.66,\n59\t      \"qwen_to_llama_coverage\": 0.52,\n60\t      \"qwen_to_llama_retention_pct\": 0.68,\n61\t      \"mean_cross_transfer_retention\": 0.68,\n62\t      \"projection_head_params\": 20971520\n63\t    },\n64\t    \"tables\": [\n65\t      \"Table: Transfer matrix (train model × test model) with coverage and retention %\"\n66\t    ],\n67\t    \"figures\": [],\n68\t    \"analysis\": \"68% mean retention demonstrates that temporal probes learn a partially universal safety commitment signature. The transfer is asymmetric (Llama→Qwen slightly better than Qwen→Llama) possibly because Llama's lower-dimensional space forces more compressed representations that transfer better. This result strengthens the cross-architecture narrative: even zero-shot transfer achieves meaningful detection, and architecture-specific fine-tuning only provides incremental improvement.\\n\\nCross-family transfer retains ~68% performance because the safety commitment signal is a shared abstract pattern (declining safety reasoning density) rather than architecture-specific feature directions. The projection head (4096→5120 linear map, ~21M params) successfully aligns representation spaces because both model families underwent similar safety fine-tuning producing structurally analogous safety subspaces. Retention at 68% (not 100%) reflects residual architectural differences in how safety reasoning is encoded at the feature level, but the temporal pattern remains transferable. This is strong evidence that safety commitment is a genuine cognitive phenomenon, not a model-specific artifact.\"\n69\t  },\n70\t  {\n71\t    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across 32B models\",\n72\t    \"description\": \"GRU multi-layer probe on 32B models. Report lead time distributions. Under best tier, temporal LR still has negative lead time but GRU shows improvement.\",\n73\t    \"results\": {\n74\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 5.2,\n75\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_median\": 1,\n76\t      \"r1_32b_gru_early_detection_rate\": 0.42,\n77\t      \"r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.79,\n78\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 4.9,\n79\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_median\": 0,\n80\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_early_detection_rate\": 0.4,\n81\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.77,\n82\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean\": -0.6,\n83\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean\": -0.9\n84\t    },\n85\t    \"tables\": [\n86\t      \"Table: Lead time statistics (mean/median/std/early_detection_rate) across all models for GRU and temporal LR\"\n87\t    ],\n88\t    \"figures\": [],\n89\t    \"analysis\": \"GRU lead time replicates cross-architecture with similar characteristics: positive mean but unreliable (high variance, low early detection rate). This confirms that the limitation is methodological rather than representational — the hidden states contain early commitment signal (as shown by oracle's +31 steps) but sequential models fail to extract it reliably. Sets up BOCPD (exp-014) as the methodological solution.\\n\\nGRU on 32B models achieves slightly better lead time than r1-8b (+5.2 vs +4.7) because 32B models have longer reasoning traces with more gradual commitment transitions, giving sequential models more signal to detect early shifts. However, median remains 0-1 and early detection rate 40-42% — the fundamental limitation persists: hidden states encode current safety state reliably but future trajectory prediction requires the BOCPD approach (exp-014) to unlock. The improvement over r1-8b is modest because the bottleneck is methodological (GRU extrapolation), not representational.\"\n90\t  },\n91\t  {\n92\t    \"name\": \"TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline\",\n93\t    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance anomaly detection (TrajGuard-style) using safe traces to estimate class-conditional Gaussians. Evaluate per-step MD as commitment detector on all 3 models.\",\n94\t    \"results\": {\n95\t      \"r1_8b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.35,\n96\t      \"r1_8b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.58,\n97\t      \"r1_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.32,\n98\t      \"r1_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.56,\n99\t      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.3,\n100\t      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.55,\n101\t      \"mean_trajguard_coverage\": 0.32,\n102\t      \"best_trajguard_coverage\": 0.35\n103\t    },\n104\t    \"tables\": [\n105\t      \"Table: TrajGuard coverage/AUROC vs temporal LR vs static LR across all models\"\n106\t    ],\n107\t    \"figures\": [],\n108\t    \"analysis\": \"TrajGuard's failure at 0.30-0.35 coverage (vs temporal LR at 0.74-0.77) quantifies the inadequacy of geometric methods. The result is not that hidden states lack signal — Fisher ratio 17-19 proves abundant signal exists — but that the signal's distribution across minor components defeats methods that rely on dominant variance directions. This validates our core insight: temporal context over supervised probes, not geometric distance in representation space, is the correct approach for reasoning-model safety detection.\\n\\nMahalanobis distance fails because it relies on the assumption that class separation is captured by the covariance structure (top principal components). Our PCA analysis (diag_d2) proved Fisher drops from 17→1 in PCA-3D — the safety signal lives in minor components that MD effectively ignores. With variance explained by top-3 PCs at only 14-17%, the Gaussian assumption in high dimensions assigns similar MD to safe and unsafe steps. Coverage 0.30-0.35 is near chance for binary detection. Larger models perform slightly worse because their 5120-d space distributes signal even more diffusely across dimensions. This decisively confirms that geometric methods designed for instruction-tuned models (where refusal is a dominant direction) are inadequate for reasoning-model safety commitment.\"\n109\t  },\n110\t  {\n111\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics\",\n112\t    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across all models. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, per-fold CV variance.\",\n113\t    \"results\": {\n114\t      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width_r1_8b\": 0.06,\n115\t      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width_r1_32b\": 0.05,\n116\t      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width_qwq\": 0.07,\n117\t      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.89,\n118\t      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_32b\": 0.91,\n119\t      \"temporal_lr_auroc_qwq\": 0.88,\n120\t      \"static_lr_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.82,\n121\t      \"bocpd_lead_ci_width\": 2.1,\n122\t      \"cv_fold_variance_coverage\": 0.003,\n123\t      \"mean_bootstrap_ci_width_coverage\": 0.06\n124\t    },\n125\t    \"tables\": [\n126\t      \"Table: All metrics with 95% bootstrap CIs across models\",\n127\t      \"Table: Cross-validation per-fold metrics (mean ± std)\"\n128\t    ],\n129\t    \"figures\": [],\n130\t    \"analysis\": \"CI widths uniformly below 0.08 confirm all primary claims are statistically robust. The temporal-static coverage gap (25-35pp) is 4-6× larger than CI width, ruling out sampling variance as explanation. AUROC values (0.88-0.91) demonstrate probe quality independent of threshold. Results support confident claim-making at EMNLP review standards.\\n\\nBootstrap CI widths of 0.05-0.07 for coverage reflect the moderate sample size (43 test traces for r1-8b, similar for 32B models). The CIs are tight enough to confirm statistical significance of the temporal-static gap (35pp gap with ~6pp CI width → non-overlapping intervals). AUROC 0.88-0.91 for temporal LR confirms strong discrimination independent of threshold choice. CV fold variance at 0.003 indicates stable probe training despite small dataset. Larger 32B models show slightly tighter CIs due to longer traces providing more per-trace signal. These intervals validate that all reported coverage differences are statistically meaningful, not noise.\"\n131\t  },\n132\t  {\n133\t    \"name\": \"Window size ablation\",\n134\t    \"description\": \"Temporal LR with W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models. Characterize diminishing returns.\",\n135\t    \"results\": {\n136\t      \"r1_8b_W1_coverage\": 0.465,\n137\t      \"r1_8b_W3_coverage\": 0.56,\n138\t      \"r1_8b_W5_coverage\": 0.65,\n139\t      \"r1_8b_W10_coverage\": 0.72,\n140\t      \"r1_8b_W15_coverage\": 0.767,\n141\t      \"r1_8b_W20_coverage\": 0.77,\n142\t      \"r1_8b_W25_coverage\": 0.76,\n143\t      \"r1_32b_W15_coverage\": 0.77,\n144\t      \"r1_32b_W10_coverage\": 0.71,\n145\t      \"qwq_W15_coverage\": 0.74,\n146\t      \"optimal_W_all_models\": 15,\n147\t      \"saturation_onset_W\": 10\n148\t    },\n149\t    \"tables\": [\n150\t      \"Table: Coverage × window size × model matrix\"\n151\t    ],\n152\t    \"figures\": [],\n153\t    \"analysis\": \"Optimal W=15 across all architectures is a key structural insight: the commitment transition unfolds over ~15 steps regardless of model. This matches the oracle's mean longest pre-CP streak (14.6 steps), providing a mechanistic explanation. The ablation transforms W=15 from arbitrary hyperparameter to principled choice grounded in task structure.\\n\\nW=1 equals static probe (0.465). Coverage increases monotonically until W=15 then plateaus/slightly decreases at W=20-25. Saturation at W=15 corresponds to the mean pre-CP safety reasoning streak length (14.6 steps from oracle analysis). Beyond W=15, the window includes steps before safety reasoning decline began, adding noise rather than signal. The consistency of optimal W=15 across architectures confirms this is determined by the task structure (commitment point transition dynamics) not model properties. Slight degradation at W=25 occurs because very long windows dilute the commitment signal with early-trace safety reasoning that is not predictive.\"\n154\t  },\n155\t  {\n156\t    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation for 32B temporal probes\",\n157\t    \"description\": \"Full layer sweep for temporal probe (not just best static layer) on 32B models. Confirm middle-layer finding.\",\n158\t    \"results\": {\n159\t      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_layer\": 30,\n160\t      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_layer_depth_pct\": 0.47,\n161\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_at_best\": 0.78,\n162\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_at_layer_16\": 0.68,\n163\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_at_layer_48\": 0.62,\n164\t      \"qwq_best_temporal_layer\": 29,\n165\t      \"qwq_best_temporal_layer_depth_pct\": 0.45,\n166\t      \"layer_range_above_70pct\": \"layers 24-36\"\n167\t    },\n168\t    \"tables\": [\n169\t      \"Table: Top-5 temporal probe layers per model with coverage/accuracy\"\n170\t    ],\n171\t    \"figures\": [],\n172\t    \"analysis\": \"Middle-layer optimality generalizes from static to temporal probes and from 32-layer to 64-layer models. The broad plateau (layers 24-36 all > 70% coverage) means layer selection is not fragile — any middle layer works reasonably. This robustness strengthens the practical deployment story.\\n\\nBest temporal layer at 45-47% depth replicates the static probe finding, confirming middle layers are optimal for both static and temporal probes. The plateau from layers 24-36 (37-56% depth) shows the safety signal is broadly available in middle layers with a gentle optimum. Early layers (< layer 16) lack sufficient abstraction; late layers (> layer 48) are too specialized for next-token prediction. The consistency of ~45% depth across architectures and probe types suggests a universal property of transformer safety representations: middle layers encode the richest task-level abstractions before output specialization.\"\n173\t  },\n174\t  {\n175\t    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n176\t    \"description\": \"Replace binary commitment label with 16-category safety reasoning taxonomy. Train multi-class temporal probe, derive commitment detection from class predictions.\",\n177\t    \"results\": {\n178\t      \"taxonomy_temporal_coverage\": 0.79,\n179\t      \"taxonomy_temporal_lead_mean\": 0.3,\n180\t      \"taxonomy_vs_binary_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n181\t      \"taxonomy_16class_accuracy\": 0.62,\n182\t      \"taxonomy_4class_accuracy\": 0.74,\n183\t      \"taxonomy_binary_derived_accuracy\": 0.85\n184\t    },\n185\t    \"tables\": [\n186\t      \"Table: Coverage/lead-time comparison: binary vs 4-class vs 16-class derived detection\"\n187\t    ],\n188\t    \"figures\": [],\n189\t    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained supervision provides marginal quantitative improvement but significant qualitative value: the taxonomy probe can report which type of safety reasoning is declining, enabling more informative alerts. For the paper, this addresses the reviewer concern but confirms binary labeling is near-optimal for detection.\\n\\nFine-grained labels provide marginal improvement (+2pp coverage) because the binary commitment label already captures the essential transition. The 16-class probe achieves only 62% accuracy on fine categories (many are rare and overlapping) but when collapsed to binary commitment detection, slightly outperforms the direct binary probe. The modest improvement suggests the binary label is already a near-sufficient supervision signal for the commitment detection task. The primary value is interpretability (knowing which safety reasoning type is declining) rather than detection performance.\"\n190\t  },\n191\t  {\n192\t    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition analysis\",\n193\t    \"description\": \"Analyze why oracle achieves +31 lead time but probes achieve +4.7. Decompose oracle advantage into components: label pattern recognition vs future prediction.\",\n194\t    \"results\": {\n195\t      \"oracle_advantage_from_lookback\": 0.45,\n196\t      \"oracle_advantage_from_future_knowledge\": 0.55,\n197\t      \"probe_captures_of_lookback_signal\": 0.72,\n198\t      \"probe_captures_of_future_signal\": 0.08,\n199\t      \"mean_pre_cp_safety_gap\": 8.3,\n200\t      \"traces_with_clear_precursor_pattern\": 0.65\n201\t    },\n202\t    \"tables\": [\n203\t      \"Table: Oracle advantage decomposition (lookback vs future knowledge)\"\n204\t    ],\n205\t    \"figures\": [],\n206\t    \"analysis\": \"The decomposition reveals a principled ceiling for non-oracle methods: ~45% of oracle's advantage is achievable from past data alone. BOCPD's success (exp-014) in reaching +8 mean lead time captures most of this achievable component via distributional shift detection. The remaining 55% requires genuine future prediction — a fundamental limitation, not a model capacity issue. This framing positions the unreliable-lead-time finding as a principled result rather than a failure.\\n\\nOracle's +31 lead time decomposes into two components: (1) lookback pattern recognition (last K steps have declining safety density — 45% of advantage, achievable from past data) and (2) future knowledge (knowing no safety labels will ever return — 55% of advantage, requires oracle access). Probes capture 72% of the lookback component but only 8% of the future component. This explains why GRU achieves +4.7 (captures most of the achievable-from-past signal) but cannot reach +31 (would require genuine future prediction). The 65% of traces with clear precursor patterns explains BOCPD's success — distributional shift detection can identify the onset of safety reasoning decline without needing future knowledge.\"\n207\t  },\n208\t  {\n209\t    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\",\n210\t    \"description\": \"4th model for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Qwen2.5-7B-base model with reasoning training. Complements Llama-8B for scale-matched comparison.\",\n211\t    \"results\": {\n212\t      \"openthinker_best_layer\": 14,\n213\t      \"openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.44,\n214\t      \"openthinker_static_coverage\": 0.41,\n215\t      \"openthinker_temporal_coverage\": 0.72,\n216\t      \"openthinker_coverage_gap_pp\": 31,\n217\t      \"openthinker_gru_lead_mean\": 4.1,\n218\t      \"scale_effect_qwen_7b_vs_32b_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n219\t      \"arch_effect_llama_vs_qwen_at_7b_coverage_delta\": -0.05\n220\t    },\n221\t    \"tables\": [\n222\t      \"Table: 2×2 matrix (arch × scale) with coverage, gap, lead time\"\n223\t    ],\n224\t    \"figures\": [],\n225\t    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix provides the definitive cross-architecture argument: temporal context (31pp effect) dwarfs both scale (2pp) and architecture (5pp) effects. This means our findings generalize broadly — any sufficiently-trained reasoning model will exhibit the same temporal advantage pattern.\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, 32 layers) shows best layer at 44% depth (layer 14/32), matching r1-8b exactly. Temporal coverage 0.72 is slightly below 32B Qwen models (0.74-0.77), showing minimal scale effect (+2pp for 4.5× parameters). Architecture effect is similarly small (Llama 0.767 vs Qwen 0.72 at matched 7-8B scale). This confirms the temporal advantage is driven by task structure, not model capacity or architecture. The 2×2 matrix shows: scale accounts for ~2pp, architecture for ~5pp, while temporal context accounts for 31pp — temporal context dominates.\"\n226\t  },\n227\t  {\n228\t    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\",\n229\t    \"description\": \"GRU-256 multi-layer probe on 32B models. If no improvement over temporal LR for coverage, strengthens 'simplicity is optimal' narrative.\",\n230\t    \"results\": {\n231\t      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.79,\n232\t      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n233\t      \"qwq_gru_coverage\": 0.76,\n234\t      \"qwq_gru_vs_lr_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n235\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 5.2,\n236\t      \"qwq_gru_lead_mean\": 4.9,\n237\t      \"attention_probe_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.78,\n238\t      \"attention_vs_gru_delta\": -0.01\n239\t    },\n240\t    \"tables\": [\n241\t      \"Table: Coverage/lead-time comparison across probe architectures on 32B models\"\n242\t    ],\n243\t    \"figures\": [],\n244\t    \"analysis\": \"The near-identical coverage between LR, GRU, and attention probes on 32B models confirms temporal LR as the optimal cost-performance tradeoff for deployment. Complex architectures add lead time capability but not coverage. This supports positioning temporal LR as the practical recommendation and BOCPD as the timing innovation.\\n\\nGRU and attention probes provide only marginal coverage improvement (+2pp) over temporal LR on 32B models, confirming that probe complexity beyond temporal windowing yields diminishing returns for coverage. The coverage ceiling (~77-79%) appears to be a property of the task (some traces lack clean commitment transitions) rather than probe capacity. GRU's value remains primarily in lead time (+5.2 steps vs -0.6 for LR), not coverage. Attention probe performs comparably to GRU, suggesting the sequential inductive bias isn't critical — temporal context itself is the key ingredient. This strongly supports the 'simplicity is optimal for coverage' narrative.\"\n245\t  },\n246\t  {\n247\t    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence-BERT + temporal LR)\",\n248\t    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings of CoT text steps + temporal LR W=15. Direct comparison to hidden-state probes. Must demonstrate hidden states provide unique value.\",\n249\t    \"results\": {\n250\t      \"text_temporal_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.48,\n251\t      \"text_temporal_coverage_r1_32b\": 0.45,\n252\t      \"text_temporal_coverage_qwq\": 0.47,\n253\t      \"text_static_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.38,\n254\t      \"text_temporal_lead_mean_r1_8b\": -3.2,\n255\t      \"text_temporal_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.76,\n256\t      \"hidden_vs_text_gap_pp_r1_8b\": 29,\n257\t      \"hidden_vs_text_gap_pp_r1_32b\": 32,\n258\t      \"hidden_vs_text_gap_pp_mean\": 30,\n259\t      \"text_temporal_gap_over_text_static_pp\": 10\n260\t    },\n261\t    \"tables\": [\n262\t      \"Table: Hidden-state vs text-based probes: coverage, lead time, AUROC across all models\",\n263\t      \"Table: Text static vs text temporal (showing temporal context helps but is insufficient)\"\n264\t    ],\n265\t    \"figures\": [],\n266\t    \"analysis\": \"The 29-32pp gap decisively proves hidden-state monitoring adds unique value over text-based approaches. This is the single most important result for paper motivation: cheap text monitoring is fundamentally inferior because the commitment signal exists in hidden states before it manifests textually. The negative text lead time (-3.2) vs positive hidden-state lead time (+4.7 GRU, +8 BOCPD) quantifies the pre-textual advantage. This validates the paper's core thesis and title emphasis on 'before it appears in text'.\\n\\nText-based temporal LR achieves only 0.45-0.48 coverage because sentence-BERT captures semantic content but misses the pre-textual safety commitment signal encoded in hidden states. Specifically: (1) hidden states encode safety orientation 2-5 steps before it manifests as explicit safety text, creating a fundamental timing advantage; (2) hidden states capture fine-grained confidence/uncertainty signals absent from surface text; (3) sentence-BERT's fixed 384-d representation compresses away subtle safety-relevant nuances that 4096/5120-d hidden states preserve. Text temporal LR does improve over text static (+10pp), confirming temporal context helps even for text — but the 29-32pp gap to hidden-state temporal LR proves hidden states carry unique pre-textual commitment signal. Negative lead time (-3.2) for text is expected: text can only react after commitment manifests linguistically.\"\n267\t  },\n268\t  {\n269\t    \"name\": \"BOCPD on probe score sequences\",\n270\t    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to temporal LR confidence scores. Detects distributional shift in probe outputs as commitment signal. Also test CUSUM.\",\n271\t    \"results\": {\n272\t      \"bocpd_lead_mean_r1_8b\": 8.2,\n273\t      \"bocpd_lead_median_r1_8b\": 6,\n274\t      \"bocpd_early_detection_rate_r1_8b\": 0.62,\n275\t      \"bocpd_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.72,\n276\t      \"bocpd_lead_mean_r1_32b\": 9.1,\n277\t      \"bocpd_lead_median_r1_32b\": 7,\n278\t      \"bocpd_early_detection_rate_r1_32b\": 0.65,\n279\t      \"bocpd_lead_mean_qwq\": 7.8,\n280\t      \"bocpd_early_detection_rate_qwq\": 0.58,\n281\t      \"bocpd_mean_lead_all_models\": 8.4,\n282\t      \"bocpd_mean_early_detection_rate\": 0.62,\n283\t      \"cusum_lead_mean_r1_8b\": 5.1,\n284\t      \"cusum_early_detection_rate_r1_8b\": 0.48,\n285\t      \"bocpd_vs_gru_lead_improvement\": 3.7,\n286\t      \"bocpd_hazard_rate\": 0.01,\n287\t      \"bocpd_observation_model\": \"gaussian\"\n288\t    },\n289\t    \"tables\": [\n290\t      \"Table: BOCPD vs GRU vs temporal LR: lead time, early detection rate, coverage across models\",\n291\t      \"Table: BOCPD hyperparameter sensitivity (hazard rate, observation model)\"\n292\t    ],\n293\t    \"figures\": [],\n294\t    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD emerges as the paper's primary methods contribution. By reframing commitment detection as online change-point detection on probe score sequences, we achieve the first reliable pre-textual detection (+8.4 mean lead, 62% early detection). The insight is that probe scores undergo a detectable regime change (high variance → low variance + high mean) several steps before textual commitment. This is genuinely novel: no prior work applies BOCPD to hidden-state probe outputs for safety monitoring. BOCPD + temporal LR probes form a complete pipeline: temporal LR provides high-coverage commitment detection, BOCPD provides reliable early warning. This upgrades Claim 3 from 'unreliable' to 'promising' and gives the paper a genuine methods contribution beyond empirical study.\\n\\nBOCPD achieves +8.4 mean lead time (vs GRU's +4.7) because it detects the distributional shift in probe confidence scores — the transition from fluctuating (safety reasoning present) to monotonically high (commitment achieved) — without needing to predict future states. This is fundamentally a change-point detection problem, not a sequence prediction problem, which is why BOCPD outperforms GRU. The probe score sequence exhibits a clear regime change: pre-commitment scores oscillate (mean 0.6, std 0.15) while post-commitment scores stabilize (mean 0.85, std 0.04). BOCPD detects this variance reduction 6-9 steps before the textual commitment point because the hidden-state probe captures the onset of commitment before text manifests it. 62% early detection rate (vs GRU's 39-42%) represents a major improvement. CUSUM achieves intermediate performance (+5.1 lead) — it detects mean shift but is less sensitive to the critical variance reduction signal. Larger models show slightly better BOCPD performance due to longer, smoother commitment transitions.\"\n295\t  },\n296\t  {\n297\t    \"name\": \"False positive analysis on safe traces\",\n298\t    \"description\": \"Run all probe methods on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5, model refused successfully). Report FPR at operational threshold (coverage ≥70%), precision-recall curves.\",\n299\t    \"results\": {\n300\t      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70pct_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.06,\n301\t      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70pct_coverage_r1_32b\": 0.05,\n302\t      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70pct_coverage_qwq\": 0.07,\n303\t      \"static_lr_fpr_at_same_threshold\": 0.12,\n304\t      \"bocpd_fpr_r1_8b\": 0.04,\n305\t      \"bocpd_fpr_r1_32b\": 0.03,\n306\t      \"trajguard_fpr\": 0.18,\n307\t      \"precision_at_70pct_recall_temporal\": 0.92,\n308\t      \"precision_at_70pct_recall_bocpd\": 0.94,\n309\t      \"n_safe_traces_tested\": 85,\n310\t      \"safe_trace_mean_probe_score\": 0.35,\n311\t      \"unsafe_trace_mean_probe_score\": 0.78\n312\t    },\n313\t    \"tables\": [\n314\t      \"Table: FPR at operational threshold across all methods and models\",\n315\t      \"Table: Precision-recall at various operating points\"\n316\t    ],\n317\t    \"figures\": [],\n318\t    \"analysis\": \"FPR 0.05-0.07 at 70% coverage demonstrates deployment viability: for every 100 safe traces monitored, only 5-7 trigger false alarms. BOCPD's lower FPR (0.03-0.04) makes it the superior deployment choice when both coverage and precision matter. The 0.35 vs 0.78 mean score separation provides comfortable operating margin. These results transform the coverage metric from potentially trivial (a probe that always fires gets 100% coverage) to meaningful: our probes achieve 70-77% coverage while maintaining <10% FPR, demonstrating genuine discriminative ability.\\n\\nFPR 0.05-0.07 at operational threshold because safe traces maintain consistent safety reasoning throughout (no density decline), producing low, stable probe scores (mean 0.35) well below the commitment detection threshold. The temporal probe learns to detect sustained absence of safety reasoning — safe traces by definition maintain safety reasoning, so false positives require unusual safe-trace patterns (e.g., brief reasoning tangents that temporarily suspend safety discussion). These are rare (~5-7% of safe traces). BOCPD achieves even lower FPR (0.03-0.04) because safe traces never exhibit the variance-reduction regime change. TrajGuard's high FPR (0.18) reflects its inability to discriminate — it fires on any representation shift, which occurs naturally in long reasoning traces even without commitment. The clear separation between safe (0.35) and unsafe (0.78) mean probe scores confirms the probe captures a real phenomenon, not noise.\"\n319\t  }\n320\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"646ceb9a-b9f2-413f-a27f-f977b72f998a","uuid":"3222e6c7-ac12-4188-8db1-242c88493317","timestamp":"2026-05-16T20:55:16.515Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T203948/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture static probe (32B models)\",\n    \"description\": \"Layer sweep + static LogisticRegression on r1-32b (Qwen2.5-base, 64 layers) and QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5-base, 64 layers). Extracts all 64 layers, finds optimal via Fisher discriminant + probe accuracy, reports static coverage.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_best_layer_idx\": 28,\n      \"r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.44,\n      \"r1_32b_static_acc\": 0.824,\n      \"r1_32b_static_coverage\": 0.42,\n      \"r1_32b_static_lead_mean\": -1.8,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig\": 19.3,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3\": 1.05,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_idx\": 30,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.47,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_acc\": 0.811,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_coverage\": 0.39,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_lead_mean\": -2.3,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig\": 18.7,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3\": 0.98,\n      \"var_explained_3pc_r1_32b\": 0.14,\n      \"var_explained_3pc_qwq\": 0.15\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Layer sweep summary (top-5 layers per model by probe accuracy, with Fisher ratios)\",\n      \"Table: Static probe metrics comparison across all 3 models (r1-8b, r1-32b, QwQ-32B)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Static probes replicate the r1-8b pattern on Qwen2.5-base architecture: high Fisher discriminant in full representation space but collapse in low-dimensional projections. The ~44% depth optimum generalizes, consistent with middle layers encoding task-relevant abstractions before output-head specialization. Static coverage 0.39-0.42 confirms Claim 1 cross-architecture: single-step linear probes are fundamentally limited to <50% coverage regardless of model scale or architecture family.\\n\\nBest layer at ~44% depth replicates r1-8b finding (layer 14/32 = 44%). Static coverage 0.39-0.42 mirrors r1-8b's 0.465 with slight degradation expected from larger model's more distributed representations. Fisher discriminant remains high in full space (18-19) but collapses in PCA-3D (~1.0), confirming the safety signal is distributed across minor principal components regardless of architecture. Qwen2.5-base models show same phenomenon as Llama-base r1-8b because the distributional property is a consequence of safety fine-tuning spreading signal across dimensions, not architecture-specific.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Temporal LR (W=15) on best-layer hidden states from r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Validates that +30pp coverage gap over static replicates cross-architecture.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.77,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.856,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.6,\n      \"r1_32b_coverage_gap_over_static_pp\": 35,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_coverage\": 0.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_acc\": 0.843,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.9,\n      \"qwq_32b_coverage_gap_over_static_pp\": 35,\n      \"mean_cross_arch_temporal_coverage\": 0.755,\n      \"mean_cross_arch_gap_pp\": 35\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Temporal vs static coverage/accuracy/lead-time across all 3 models\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ≥25pp temporal advantage replicates on both Qwen2.5-base models, confirming Claim 2 is architecture-general. Coverage values (0.74-0.77) cluster near the r1-8b oracle ceiling (76.7%), suggesting the ~77% bound may reflect an intrinsic property of the commitment detection task (some traces have ambiguous/gradual commitment without clean phase transition). The result validates that temporal context — not model capacity or architecture — is the critical missing ingredient for static probes.\\n\\nTemporal window W=15 captures 'safety reasoning density decline' — the gradual thinning of safety-labeled steps before commitment point. This pattern is architectural-invariant because it arises from the shared safety fine-tuning objective: all models learn to reason about safety similarly, producing similar temporal density patterns. The +35pp gap over static probes occurs because the commitment signature is fundamentally a trajectory property (declining safety density over 10-15 steps), not a single-step state property. r1-32b slightly outperforms QwQ due to deeper safety fine-tuning in the DeepSeek-R1 pipeline, but both saturate near the oracle ceiling (76.7% on r1-8b), suggesting the oracle bound is also architecture-invariant.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer (Llama↔Qwen)\",\n    \"description\": \"Train temporal probe on r1-8b (Llama-3.1, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5, 5120-d) via learned linear projection head. Also reverse direction.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"llama_to_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.54,\n      \"llama_to_r1_32b_retention_pct\": 0.7,\n      \"llama_to_qwq_coverage\": 0.51,\n      \"llama_to_qwq_retention_pct\": 0.66,\n      \"qwen_to_llama_coverage\": 0.52,\n      \"qwen_to_llama_retention_pct\": 0.68,\n      \"mean_cross_transfer_retention\": 0.68,\n      \"projection_head_params\": 20971520\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Transfer matrix (train model × test model) with coverage and retention %\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"68% mean retention demonstrates that temporal probes learn a partially universal safety commitment signature. The transfer is asymmetric (Llama→Qwen slightly better than Qwen→Llama) possibly because Llama's lower-dimensional space forces more compressed representations that transfer better. This result strengthens the cross-architecture narrative: even zero-shot transfer achieves meaningful detection, and architecture-specific fine-tuning only provides incremental improvement.\\n\\nCross-family transfer retains ~68% performance because the safety commitment signal is a shared abstract pattern (declining safety reasoning density) rather than architecture-specific feature directions. The projection head (4096→5120 linear map, ~21M params) successfully aligns representation spaces because both model families underwent similar safety fine-tuning producing structurally analogous safety subspaces. Retention at 68% (not 100%) reflects residual architectural differences in how safety reasoning is encoded at the feature level, but the temporal pattern remains transferable. This is strong evidence that safety commitment is a genuine cognitive phenomenon, not a model-specific artifact.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"GRU multi-layer probe on 32B models. Report lead time distributions. Under best tier, temporal LR still has negative lead time but GRU shows improvement.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 5.2,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_median\": 1,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_early_detection_rate\": 0.42,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.79,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 4.9,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_median\": 0,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_early_detection_rate\": 0.4,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.77,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean\": -0.6,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean\": -0.9\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Lead time statistics (mean/median/std/early_detection_rate) across all models for GRU and temporal LR\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"GRU lead time replicates cross-architecture with similar characteristics: positive mean but unreliable (high variance, low early detection rate). This confirms that the limitation is methodological rather than representational — the hidden states contain early commitment signal (as shown by oracle's +31 steps) but sequential models fail to extract it reliably. Sets up BOCPD (exp-014) as the methodological solution.\\n\\nGRU on 32B models achieves slightly better lead time than r1-8b (+5.2 vs +4.7) because 32B models have longer reasoning traces with more gradual commitment transitions, giving sequential models more signal to detect early shifts. However, median remains 0-1 and early detection rate 40-42% — the fundamental limitation persists: hidden states encode current safety state reliably but future trajectory prediction requires the BOCPD approach (exp-014) to unlock. The improvement over r1-8b is modest because the bottleneck is methodological (GRU extrapolation), not representational.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance anomaly detection (TrajGuard-style) using safe traces to estimate class-conditional Gaussians. Evaluate per-step MD as commitment detector on all 3 models.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.35,\n      \"r1_8b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.58,\n      \"r1_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.32,\n      \"r1_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.56,\n      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_coverage\": 0.3,\n      \"qwq_32b_trajguard_auroc\": 0.55,\n      \"mean_trajguard_coverage\": 0.32,\n      \"best_trajguard_coverage\": 0.35\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: TrajGuard coverage/AUROC vs temporal LR vs static LR across all models\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"TrajGuard's failure at 0.30-0.35 coverage (vs temporal LR at 0.74-0.77) quantifies the inadequacy of geometric methods. The result is not that hidden states lack signal — Fisher ratio 17-19 proves abundant signal exists — but that the signal's distribution across minor components defeats methods that rely on dominant variance directions. This validates our core insight: temporal context over supervised probes, not geometric distance in representation space, is the correct approach for reasoning-model safety detection.\\n\\nMahalanobis distance fails because it relies on the assumption that class separation is captured by the covariance structure (top principal components). Our PCA analysis (diag_d2) proved Fisher drops from 17→1 in PCA-3D — the safety signal lives in minor components that MD effectively ignores. With variance explained by top-3 PCs at only 14-17%, the Gaussian assumption in high dimensions assigns similar MD to safe and unsafe steps. Coverage 0.30-0.35 is near chance for binary detection. Larger models perform slightly worse because their 5120-d space distributes signal even more diffusely across dimensions. This decisively confirms that geometric methods designed for instruction-tuned models (where refusal is a dominant direction) are inadequate for reasoning-model safety commitment.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics\",\n    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across all models. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, per-fold CV variance.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width_r1_8b\": 0.06,\n      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width_r1_32b\": 0.05,\n      \"temporal_lr_coverage_ci_width_qwq\": 0.07,\n      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.89,\n      \"temporal_lr_auroc_r1_32b\": 0.91,\n      \"temporal_lr_auroc_qwq\": 0.88,\n      \"static_lr_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.82,\n      \"bocpd_lead_ci_width\": 2.1,\n      \"cv_fold_variance_coverage\": 0.003,\n      \"mean_bootstrap_ci_width_coverage\": 0.06\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: All metrics with 95% bootstrap CIs across models\",\n      \"Table: Cross-validation per-fold metrics (mean ± std)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"CI widths uniformly below 0.08 confirm all primary claims are statistically robust. The temporal-static coverage gap (25-35pp) is 4-6× larger than CI width, ruling out sampling variance as explanation. AUROC values (0.88-0.91) demonstrate probe quality independent of threshold. Results support confident claim-making at EMNLP review standards.\\n\\nBootstrap CI widths of 0.05-0.07 for coverage reflect the moderate sample size (43 test traces for r1-8b, similar for 32B models). The CIs are tight enough to confirm statistical significance of the temporal-static gap (35pp gap with ~6pp CI width → non-overlapping intervals). AUROC 0.88-0.91 for temporal LR confirms strong discrimination independent of threshold choice. CV fold variance at 0.003 indicates stable probe training despite small dataset. Larger 32B models show slightly tighter CIs due to longer traces providing more per-trace signal. These intervals validate that all reported coverage differences are statistically meaningful, not noise.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Window size ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Temporal LR with W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models. Characterize diminishing returns.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_W1_coverage\": 0.465,\n      \"r1_8b_W3_coverage\": 0.56,\n      \"r1_8b_W5_coverage\": 0.65,\n      \"r1_8b_W10_coverage\": 0.72,\n      \"r1_8b_W15_coverage\": 0.767,\n      \"r1_8b_W20_coverage\": 0.77,\n      \"r1_8b_W25_coverage\": 0.76,\n      \"r1_32b_W15_coverage\": 0.77,\n      \"r1_32b_W10_coverage\": 0.71,\n      \"qwq_W15_coverage\": 0.74,\n      \"optimal_W_all_models\": 15,\n      \"saturation_onset_W\": 10\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Coverage × window size × model matrix\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Optimal W=15 across all architectures is a key structural insight: the commitment transition unfolds over ~15 steps regardless of model. This matches the oracle's mean longest pre-CP streak (14.6 steps), providing a mechanistic explanation. The ablation transforms W=15 from arbitrary hyperparameter to principled choice grounded in task structure.\\n\\nW=1 equals static probe (0.465). Coverage increases monotonically until W=15 then plateaus/slightly decreases at W=20-25. Saturation at W=15 corresponds to the mean pre-CP safety reasoning streak length (14.6 steps from oracle analysis). Beyond W=15, the window includes steps before safety reasoning decline began, adding noise rather than signal. The consistency of optimal W=15 across architectures confirms this is determined by the task structure (commitment point transition dynamics) not model properties. Slight degradation at W=25 occurs because very long windows dilute the commitment signal with early-trace safety reasoning that is not predictive.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation for 32B temporal probes\",\n    \"description\": \"Full layer sweep for temporal probe (not just best static layer) on 32B models. Confirm middle-layer finding.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_layer\": 30,\n      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_layer_depth_pct\": 0.47,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_at_best\": 0.78,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_at_layer_16\": 0.68,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_at_layer_48\": 0.62,\n      \"qwq_best_temporal_layer\": 29,\n      \"qwq_best_temporal_layer_depth_pct\": 0.45,\n      \"layer_range_above_70pct\": \"layers 24-36\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Top-5 temporal probe layers per model with coverage/accuracy\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Middle-layer optimality generalizes from static to temporal probes and from 32-layer to 64-layer models. The broad plateau (layers 24-36 all > 70% coverage) means layer selection is not fragile — any middle layer works reasonably. This robustness strengthens the practical deployment story.\\n\\nBest temporal layer at 45-47% depth replicates the static probe finding, confirming middle layers are optimal for both static and temporal probes. The plateau from layers 24-36 (37-56% depth) shows the safety signal is broadly available in middle layers with a gentle optimum. Early layers (< layer 16) lack sufficient abstraction; late layers (> layer 48) are too specialized for next-token prediction. The consistency of ~45% depth across architectures and probe types suggests a universal property of transformer safety representations: middle layers encode the richest task-level abstractions before output specialization.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n    \"description\": \"Replace binary commitment label with 16-category safety reasoning taxonomy. Train multi-class temporal probe, derive commitment detection from class predictions.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"taxonomy_temporal_coverage\": 0.79,\n      \"taxonomy_temporal_lead_mean\": 0.3,\n      \"taxonomy_vs_binary_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n      \"taxonomy_16class_accuracy\": 0.62,\n      \"taxonomy_4class_accuracy\": 0.74,\n      \"taxonomy_binary_derived_accuracy\": 0.85\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Coverage/lead-time comparison: binary vs 4-class vs 16-class derived detection\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained supervision provides marginal quantitative improvement but significant qualitative value: the taxonomy probe can report which type of safety reasoning is declining, enabling more informative alerts. For the paper, this addresses the reviewer concern but confirms binary labeling is near-optimal for detection.\\n\\nFine-grained labels provide marginal improvement (+2pp coverage) because the binary commitment label already captures the essential transition. The 16-class probe achieves only 62% accuracy on fine categories (many are rare and overlapping) but when collapsed to binary commitment detection, slightly outperforms the direct binary probe. The modest improvement suggests the binary label is already a near-sufficient supervision signal for the commitment detection task. The primary value is interpretability (knowing which safety reasoning type is declining) rather than detection performance.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Analyze why oracle achieves +31 lead time but probes achieve +4.7. Decompose oracle advantage into components: label pattern recognition vs future prediction.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"oracle_advantage_from_lookback\": 0.45,\n      \"oracle_advantage_from_future_knowledge\": 0.55,\n      \"probe_captures_of_lookback_signal\": 0.72,\n      \"probe_captures_of_future_signal\": 0.08,\n      \"mean_pre_cp_safety_gap\": 8.3,\n      \"traces_with_clear_precursor_pattern\": 0.65\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Oracle advantage decomposition (lookback vs future knowledge)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The decomposition reveals a principled ceiling for non-oracle methods: ~45% of oracle's advantage is achievable from past data alone. BOCPD's success (exp-014) in reaching +8 mean lead time captures most of this achievable component via distributional shift detection. The remaining 55% requires genuine future prediction — a fundamental limitation, not a model capacity issue. This framing positions the unreliable-lead-time finding as a principled result rather than a failure.\\n\\nOracle's +31 lead time decomposes into two components: (1) lookback pattern recognition (last K steps have declining safety density — 45% of advantage, achievable from past data) and (2) future knowledge (knowing no safety labels will ever return — 55% of advantage, requires oracle access). Probes capture 72% of the lookback component but only 8% of the future component. This explains why GRU achieves +4.7 (captures most of the achievable-from-past signal) but cannot reach +31 (would require genuine future prediction). The 65% of traces with clear precursor patterns explains BOCPD's success — distributional shift detection can identify the onset of safety reasoning decline without needing future knowledge.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\",\n    \"description\": \"4th model for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Qwen2.5-7B-base model with reasoning training. Complements Llama-8B for scale-matched comparison.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"openthinker_best_layer\": 14,\n      \"openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct\": 0.44,\n      \"openthinker_static_coverage\": 0.41,\n      \"openthinker_temporal_coverage\": 0.72,\n      \"openthinker_coverage_gap_pp\": 31,\n      \"openthinker_gru_lead_mean\": 4.1,\n      \"scale_effect_qwen_7b_vs_32b_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n      \"arch_effect_llama_vs_qwen_at_7b_coverage_delta\": -0.05\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: 2×2 matrix (arch × scale) with coverage, gap, lead time\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix provides the definitive cross-architecture argument: temporal context (31pp effect) dwarfs both scale (2pp) and architecture (5pp) effects. This means our findings generalize broadly — any sufficiently-trained reasoning model will exhibit the same temporal advantage pattern.\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, 32 layers) shows best layer at 44% depth (layer 14/32), matching r1-8b exactly. Temporal coverage 0.72 is slightly below 32B Qwen models (0.74-0.77), showing minimal scale effect (+2pp for 4.5× parameters). Architecture effect is similarly small (Llama 0.767 vs Qwen 0.72 at matched 7-8B scale). This confirms the temporal advantage is driven by task structure, not model capacity or architecture. The 2×2 matrix shows: scale accounts for ~2pp, architecture for ~5pp, while temporal context accounts for 31pp — temporal context dominates.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"GRU-256 multi-layer probe on 32B models. If no improvement over temporal LR for coverage, strengthens 'simplicity is optimal' narrative.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.79,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n      \"qwq_gru_coverage\": 0.76,\n      \"qwq_gru_vs_lr_coverage_delta\": 0.02,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 5.2,\n      \"qwq_gru_lead_mean\": 4.9,\n      \"attention_probe_r1_32b_coverage\": 0.78,\n      \"attention_vs_gru_delta\": -0.01\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Coverage/lead-time comparison across probe architectures on 32B models\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The near-identical coverage between LR, GRU, and attention probes on 32B models confirms temporal LR as the optimal cost-performance tradeoff for deployment. Complex architectures add lead time capability but not coverage. This supports positioning temporal LR as the practical recommendation and BOCPD as the timing innovation.\\n\\nGRU and attention probes provide only marginal coverage improvement (+2pp) over temporal LR on 32B models, confirming that probe complexity beyond temporal windowing yields diminishing returns for coverage. The coverage ceiling (~77-79%) appears to be a property of the task (some traces lack clean commitment transitions) rather than probe capacity. GRU's value remains primarily in lead time (+5.2 steps vs -0.6 for LR), not coverage. Attention probe performs comparably to GRU, suggesting the sequential inductive bias isn't critical — temporal context itself is the key ingredient. This strongly supports the 'simplicity is optimal for coverage' narrative.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence-BERT + temporal LR)\",\n    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) embeddings of CoT text steps + temporal LR W=15. Direct comparison to hidden-state probes. Must demonstrate hidden states provide unique value.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"text_temporal_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.48,\n      \"text_temporal_coverage_r1_32b\": 0.45,\n      \"text_temporal_coverage_qwq\": 0.47,\n      \"text_static_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.38,\n      \"text_temporal_lead_mean_r1_8b\": -3.2,\n      \"text_temporal_auroc_r1_8b\": 0.76,\n      \"hidden_vs_text_gap_pp_r1_8b\": 29,\n      \"hidden_vs_text_gap_pp_r1_32b\": 32,\n      \"hidden_vs_text_gap_pp_mean\": 30,\n      \"text_temporal_gap_over_text_static_pp\": 10\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Hidden-state vs text-based probes: coverage, lead time, AUROC across all models\",\n      \"Table: Text static vs text temporal (showing temporal context helps but is insufficient)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 29-32pp gap decisively proves hidden-state monitoring adds unique value over text-based approaches. This is the single most important result for paper motivation: cheap text monitoring is fundamentally inferior because the commitment signal exists in hidden states before it manifests textually. The negative text lead time (-3.2) vs positive hidden-state lead time (+4.7 GRU, +8 BOCPD) quantifies the pre-textual advantage. This validates the paper's core thesis and title emphasis on 'before it appears in text'.\\n\\nText-based temporal LR achieves only 0.45-0.48 coverage because sentence-BERT captures semantic content but misses the pre-textual safety commitment signal encoded in hidden states. Specifically: (1) hidden states encode safety orientation 2-5 steps before it manifests as explicit safety text, creating a fundamental timing advantage; (2) hidden states capture fine-grained confidence/uncertainty signals absent from surface text; (3) sentence-BERT's fixed 384-d representation compresses away subtle safety-relevant nuances that 4096/5120-d hidden states preserve. Text temporal LR does improve over text static (+10pp), confirming temporal context helps even for text — but the 29-32pp gap to hidden-state temporal LR proves hidden states carry unique pre-textual commitment signal. Negative lead time (-3.2) for text is expected: text can only react after commitment manifests linguistically.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"BOCPD on probe score sequences\",\n    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (Adams & MacKay 2007) applied to temporal LR confidence scores. Detects distributional shift in probe outputs as commitment signal. Also test CUSUM.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bocpd_lead_mean_r1_8b\": 8.2,\n      \"bocpd_lead_median_r1_8b\": 6,\n      \"bocpd_early_detection_rate_r1_8b\": 0.62,\n      \"bocpd_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.72,\n      \"bocpd_lead_mean_r1_32b\": 9.1,\n      \"bocpd_lead_median_r1_32b\": 7,\n      \"bocpd_early_detection_rate_r1_32b\": 0.65,\n      \"bocpd_lead_mean_qwq\": 7.8,\n      \"bocpd_early_detection_rate_qwq\": 0.58,\n      \"bocpd_mean_lead_all_models\": 8.4,\n      \"bocpd_mean_early_detection_rate\": 0.62,\n      \"cusum_lead_mean_r1_8b\": 5.1,\n      \"cusum_early_detection_rate_r1_8b\": 0.48,\n      \"bocpd_vs_gru_lead_improvement\": 3.7,\n      \"bocpd_hazard_rate\": 0.01,\n      \"bocpd_observation_model\": \"gaussian\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: BOCPD vs GRU vs temporal LR: lead time, early detection rate, coverage across models\",\n      \"Table: BOCPD hyperparameter sensitivity (hazard rate, observation model)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD emerges as the paper's primary methods contribution. By reframing commitment detection as online change-point detection on probe score sequences, we achieve the first reliable pre-textual detection (+8.4 mean lead, 62% early detection). The insight is that probe scores undergo a detectable regime change (high variance → low variance + high mean) several steps before textual commitment. This is genuinely novel: no prior work applies BOCPD to hidden-state probe outputs for safety monitoring. BOCPD + temporal LR probes form a complete pipeline: temporal LR provides high-coverage commitment detection, BOCPD provides reliable early warning. This upgrades Claim 3 from 'unreliable' to 'promising' and gives the paper a genuine methods contribution beyond empirical study.\\n\\nBOCPD achieves +8.4 mean lead time (vs GRU's +4.7) because it detects the distributional shift in probe confidence scores — the transition from fluctuating (safety reasoning present) to monotonically high (commitment achieved) — without needing to predict future states. This is fundamentally a change-point detection problem, not a sequence prediction problem, which is why BOCPD outperforms GRU. The probe score sequence exhibits a clear regime change: pre-commitment scores oscillate (mean 0.6, std 0.15) while post-commitment scores stabilize (mean 0.85, std 0.04). BOCPD detects this variance reduction 6-9 steps before the textual commitment point because the hidden-state probe captures the onset of commitment before text manifests it. 62% early detection rate (vs GRU's 39-42%) represents a major improvement. CUSUM achieves intermediate performance (+5.1 lead) — it detects mean shift but is less sensitive to the critical variance reduction signal. Larger models show slightly better BOCPD performance due to longer, smoother commitment transitions.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"False positive analysis on safe traces\",\n    \"description\": \"Run all probe methods on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5, model refused successfully). Report FPR at operational threshold (coverage ≥70%), precision-recall curves.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70pct_coverage_r1_8b\": 0.06,\n      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70pct_coverage_r1_32b\": 0.05,\n      \"temporal_lr_fpr_at_70pct_coverage_qwq\": 0.07,\n      \"static_lr_fpr_at_same_threshold\": 0.12,\n      \"bocpd_fpr_r1_8b\": 0.04,\n      \"bocpd_fpr_r1_32b\": 0.03,\n      \"trajguard_fpr\": 0.18,\n      \"precision_at_70pct_recall_temporal\": 0.92,\n      \"precision_at_70pct_recall_bocpd\": 0.94,\n      \"n_safe_traces_tested\": 85,\n      \"safe_trace_mean_probe_score\": 0.35,\n      \"unsafe_trace_mean_probe_score\": 0.78\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: FPR at operational threshold across all methods and models\",\n      \"Table: Precision-recall at various operating points\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"FPR 0.05-0.07 at 70% coverage demonstrates deployment viability: for every 100 safe traces monitored, only 5-7 trigger false alarms. BOCPD's lower FPR (0.03-0.04) makes it the superior deployment choice when both coverage and precision matter. The 0.35 vs 0.78 mean score separation provides comfortable operating margin. These results transform the coverage metric from potentially trivial (a probe that always fires gets 100% coverage) to meaningful: our probes achieve 70-77% coverage while maintaining <10% FPR, demonstrating genuine discriminative ability.\\n\\nFPR 0.05-0.07 at operational threshold because safe traces maintain consistent safety reasoning throughout (no density decline), producing low, stable probe scores (mean 0.35) well below the commitment detection threshold. The temporal probe learns to detect sustained absence of safety reasoning — safe traces by definition maintain safety reasoning, so false positives require unusual safe-trace patterns (e.g., brief reasoning tangents that temporarily suspend safety discussion). These are rare (~5-7% of safe traces). BOCPD achieves even lower FPR (0.03-0.04) because safe traces never exhibit the variance-reduction regime change. TrajGuard's high FPR (0.18) reflects its inability to discriminate — it fires on any representation shift, which occurs naturally in long reasoning traces even without commitment. The clear separation between safe (0.35) and unsafe (0.78) mean probe scores confirms the probe captures a real phenomenon, not noise.\"\n  }\n]","numLines":320,"startLine":1,"totalLines":320}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NPiTsshjjUCK4BitJzEoxD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FRnaZh79KBL5Ky4oMwu37L","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T203948/best/paper_framing.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11539,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11539},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"646ceb9a-b9f2-413f-a27f-f977b72f998a","uuid":"a1fc5dd7-b26f-49d9-a86b-d986a0834b61"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FRnaZh79KBL5Ky4oMwu37L","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n5\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026 (9 days)\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims (3 条)\n9\t\n10\t1. **Static hidden-state probes fail to reliably detect safety commitment in reasoning model traces** — static per-step probes (LogisticRegression, linear classifiers analogous to SafeSwitch/SIREN) achieve <50% detection coverage on the commitment detection task, despite hidden states containing strong safety-relevant signal (Fisher discriminant ratio 17-18 in full representation space).\n11\t\n12\t2. **Temporal window probes saturate oracle-achievable detection coverage** — a simple temporal window LogisticRegression (W=15 steps) achieves 76.7% detection coverage, matching the label-oracle upper bound. The +30pp improvement over static probes comes from capturing \"safety reasoning density decline\" patterns invisible to single-step classification.\n13\t\n14\t3. **Pre-textual detection of safety commitment remains unreliable despite positive mean lead time** — GRU sequence models achieve positive mean lead time (+4.7 steps) but with median 0, std 13, and only 39% (13/33) of detected traces achieving genuine early detection. The oracle's +31-step advantage (100% early detection) reveals a fundamental gap: hidden states encode current safety state well but poorly predict future safety trajectory. This establishes that reliable pre-textual detection requires architectural advances beyond current sequential models.\n15\t\n16\t## Story Spine\n17\t\n18\t- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution are interleaved throughout extended CoT (84% of traces contain safety labels after step 10), culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state jailbreak detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) are designed for instruction-tuned models where the attack is an adversarial INPUT; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the attack is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n19\t\n20\t- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense rate on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level granularity. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon but doesn't provide deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No work bridges hidden-state detection TO reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n21\t\n22\t- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of hidden-state detection methods on reasoning-model safety commitment, discovering that (a) the safety signal exists but is distributed across minor principal components (PCA top-3 explains only 17% variance, Fisher drops from 17→1), making geometric methods (Mahalanobis/TrajGuard) fail; (b) temporal context is critical — temporal window LR saturates coverage (+30pp), and GRU achieves positive lead time (+4.7 steps); (c) but pre-textual detection remains unreliable (median 0, only 39% early detection vs oracle 100%), establishing a principled gap between current capabilities and the oracle ceiling.\n23\t\n24\t- **Headline result template**: \"On HarmThoughts reasoning traces, temporal methods achieve 76.7% detection coverage (matching oracle ceiling) and +4.7 steps mean lead time, while static probes achieve only 46.5% coverage with negative lead time — but reliable pre-textual detection remains elusive (39% early detection rate vs oracle 100%, +4.7 vs +31 steps).\"\n25\t\n26\t## Venue Fit Risks\n27\t\n28\t- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection method for reasoning model jailbreak, directly addressing \"defense lags behind attack\" gap noted in track CFP.\n29\t- **Method simplicity risk**: Core methods are temporal LR (coverage) and GRU (lead time) — neither is architecturally novel. Mitigate: frame as empirical study with method insight; the contribution is the systematic evaluation + discovery of what matters (temporal context, adequate model capacity, multi-layer signal aggregation).\n30\t- **Unreliable timing risk**: Claim 3 shows GRU achieves +4.7 mean lead time but median 0 and high variance. Mitigate: frame honestly as \"proof of concept for timing but currently unreliable\" — this motivates future work while contributing the coverage + limitation analysis now.\n31\t- **Cross-architecture coverage**: MVP only tested on r1-8b (Llama-3.1 base). Must validate on Qwen2.5-based models (r1-32b, QwQ-32B) for the \"cross-architecture\" story. If temporal advantage doesn't hold cross-architecture → Claim 2 weakens to single-model.\n32\t\n33\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n34\t\n35\t1. \"Temporal window LR is trivial — where's the method novelty?\" → Primary contribution is empirical study; method insight is that simplicity matches oracle ceiling. Additionally, BOCPD on probe score sequences as a change-point detection approach adds a novel method component that could achieve positive lead time.\n36\t2. \"Only tested on one model family\" → Cross-architecture experiments (P0) will address (4 models, 2 families, 2 scales).\n37\t3. \"GRU lead time is unreliable (median 0, high variance)\" → Acknowledged; mean +4.7 shows the signal exists but is hard to extract reliably. The coverage contribution (+30pp) is robust. GRU lead time is presented as proof-of-concept, not deployed solution.\n38\t4. \"209 training traces is too few — results may not generalize\" → Cross-model validation + bootstrap CIs + cross-validation with per-fold variance reporting.\n39\t5. \"Why not use the full 16-label taxonomy for richer supervision?\" → Ablation planned (P1).\n40\t6. \"How does hidden-state monitoring compare to text-based detection?\" → CRITICAL: Must include text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text). If text achieves comparable performance, hidden-state motivation collapses. Hypothesis: hidden states capture pre-textual signal that text cannot.\n41\t7. \"Commitment point definition is circular / tautological\" → CP defined as last safety label + 1; probe detects sustained absence of safety reasoning. Mitigate: (a) discuss what CP corresponds to behaviorally, (b) report detection delay distribution showing probes fire at/after CP not just because of label definition, (c) cross-model consistency suggests real phenomenon not annotation artifact.\n42\t8. \"Missing related work on representation engineering, PRMs, change-point detection\" → Must cite: Zou et al. 2023 (RepE), Arditi et al. 2024 (refusal direction), Lightman et al. 2023 (PRMs/step-level supervision), Adams & MacKay 2007 (BOCPD), Hewitt & Liang 2019 (control tasks for probing).\n43\t9. \"No false positive analysis — deployment utility unknown\" → Must report FPR on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) and precision-recall curves alongside coverage.\n44\t\n45\t## Title Note\n46\tDrop \"Contrastive\" from title — no contrastive learning is employed. Candidate: \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\" or \"Hidden-State Detection of Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models: When Does Temporal Context Matter?\"\n47\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"646ceb9a-b9f2-413f-a27f-f977b72f998a","uuid":"782c9a19-2f24-4088-85a7-a5e64ae2a384","timestamp":"2026-05-16T20:55:16.607Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T203948/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026 (9 days)\n\n## Core Claims (3 条)\n\n1. **Static hidden-state probes fail to reliably detect safety commitment in reasoning model traces** — static per-step probes (LogisticRegression, linear classifiers analogous to SafeSwitch/SIREN) achieve <50% detection coverage on the commitment detection task, despite hidden states containing strong safety-relevant signal (Fisher discriminant ratio 17-18 in full representation space).\n\n2. **Temporal window probes saturate oracle-achievable detection coverage** — a simple temporal window LogisticRegression (W=15 steps) achieves 76.7% detection coverage, matching the label-oracle upper bound. The +30pp improvement over static probes comes from capturing \"safety reasoning density decline\" patterns invisible to single-step classification.\n\n3. **Pre-textual detection of safety commitment remains unreliable despite positive mean lead time** — GRU sequence models achieve positive mean lead time (+4.7 steps) but with median 0, std 13, and only 39% (13/33) of detected traces achieving genuine early detection. The oracle's +31-step advantage (100% early detection) reveals a fundamental gap: hidden states encode current safety state well but poorly predict future safety trajectory. This establishes that reliable pre-textual detection requires architectural advances beyond current sequential models.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution are interleaved throughout extended CoT (84% of traces contain safety labels after step 10), culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state jailbreak detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) are designed for instruction-tuned models where the attack is an adversarial INPUT; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the attack is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n\n- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense rate on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level granularity. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon but doesn't provide deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No work bridges hidden-state detection TO reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n\n- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of hidden-state detection methods on reasoning-model safety commitment, discovering that (a) the safety signal exists but is distributed across minor principal components (PCA top-3 explains only 17% variance, Fisher drops from 17→1), making geometric methods (Mahalanobis/TrajGuard) fail; (b) temporal context is critical — temporal window LR saturates coverage (+30pp), and GRU achieves positive lead time (+4.7 steps); (c) but pre-textual detection remains unreliable (median 0, only 39% early detection vs oracle 100%), establishing a principled gap between current capabilities and the oracle ceiling.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On HarmThoughts reasoning traces, temporal methods achieve 76.7% detection coverage (matching oracle ceiling) and +4.7 steps mean lead time, while static probes achieve only 46.5% coverage with negative lead time — but reliable pre-textual detection remains elusive (39% early detection rate vs oracle 100%, +4.7 vs +31 steps).\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection method for reasoning model jailbreak, directly addressing \"defense lags behind attack\" gap noted in track CFP.\n- **Method simplicity risk**: Core methods are temporal LR (coverage) and GRU (lead time) — neither is architecturally novel. Mitigate: frame as empirical study with method insight; the contribution is the systematic evaluation + discovery of what matters (temporal context, adequate model capacity, multi-layer signal aggregation).\n- **Unreliable timing risk**: Claim 3 shows GRU achieves +4.7 mean lead time but median 0 and high variance. Mitigate: frame honestly as \"proof of concept for timing but currently unreliable\" — this motivates future work while contributing the coverage + limitation analysis now.\n- **Cross-architecture coverage**: MVP only tested on r1-8b (Llama-3.1 base). Must validate on Qwen2.5-based models (r1-32b, QwQ-32B) for the \"cross-architecture\" story. If temporal advantage doesn't hold cross-architecture → Claim 2 weakens to single-model.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. \"Temporal window LR is trivial — where's the method novelty?\" → Primary contribution is empirical study; method insight is that simplicity matches oracle ceiling. Additionally, BOCPD on probe score sequences as a change-point detection approach adds a novel method component that could achieve positive lead time.\n2. \"Only tested on one model family\" → Cross-architecture experiments (P0) will address (4 models, 2 families, 2 scales).\n3. \"GRU lead time is unreliable (median 0, high variance)\" → Acknowledged; mean +4.7 shows the signal exists but is hard to extract reliably. The coverage contribution (+30pp) is robust. GRU lead time is presented as proof-of-concept, not deployed solution.\n4. \"209 training traces is too few — results may not generalize\" → Cross-model validation + bootstrap CIs + cross-validation with per-fold variance reporting.\n5. \"Why not use the full 16-label taxonomy for richer supervision?\" → Ablation planned (P1).\n6. \"How does hidden-state monitoring compare to text-based detection?\" → CRITICAL: Must include text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text). If text achieves comparable performance, hidden-state motivation collapses. Hypothesis: hidden states capture pre-textual signal that text cannot.\n7. \"Commitment point definition is circular / tautological\" → CP defined as last safety label + 1; probe detects sustained absence of safety reasoning. Mitigate: (a) discuss what CP corresponds to behaviorally, (b) report detection delay distribution showing probes fire at/after CP not just because of label definition, (c) cross-model consistency suggests real phenomenon not annotation artifact.\n8. \"Missing related work on representation engineering, PRMs, change-point detection\" → Must cite: Zou et al. 2023 (RepE), Arditi et al. 2024 (refusal direction), Lightman et al. 2023 (PRMs/step-level supervision), Adams & MacKay 2007 (BOCPD), Hewitt & Liang 2019 (control tasks for probing).\n9. \"No false positive analysis — deployment utility unknown\" → Must report FPR on safe traces (final_judgment=0.5) and precision-recall curves alongside coverage.\n\n## Title Note\nDrop \"Contrastive\" from title — no contrastive learning is employed. Candidate: \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models\" or \"Hidden-State Detection of Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models: When Does Temporal Context Matter?\"\n","numLines":47,"startLine":1,"totalLines":47}}}
